[al:新概念英语（四）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:The Stuff of Dreams]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.79]Lesson 19 | 第19课
[00:02.70]The stuff of dreams | 梦的本质
[00:10.86]What is going on when a person experiences rapid eye-movements during sleep? | 人睡眠时眼球快速移动，此时正在发生什么？
[00:18.37]It is fairly clear that the sleeping period must have some function, and because there is so much of it the function would seem to be important. | 相当清楚的是，睡眠期一定具有某种功能，而且既然睡眠占了那么多时间，其功能似乎还很重要。
[00:28.71]Speculations about its nature have been going on for literally thousands of years, | 关于睡眠的性质，人们确实已经推测了数千年，
[00:35.16]and one odd finding that makes the problem puzzling is that it looks very much as if sleeping is not simply a matter of giving the body a rest. | 而使这个问题令人困惑的一个奇怪的发现是，睡眠看起来很不像单纯让身体休息一下的问题。
[00:45.36]'Rest', in terms of muscle relaxation and so on, can be achieved by a brief period lying, or even sitting down. | “休息”，就肌肉放松等而言，躺一小会儿，或者甚至坐一会儿就能达到。
[00:55.75]The body's tissues are self-repairing and self-restoring to a degree, and function best when more or less continuously active. | 人体组织在一定程度上能自行修复和自行恢复，而且或多或少持续活动时功能最佳。
[01:06.27]In fact a basic amount of movement occurs during sleep which is specifically concerned with preventing muscle inactivity. | 事实上，睡眠期间有一定量的活动，其目的正是防止肌肉不活动。
[01:15.66]If it is not a question of resting the body, then perhaps it is the brain that needs resting? | 如果问题不在于让身体休息，那么也许是大脑需要休息？
[01:23.65]This might be a plausible hypothesis were it not for two factors. | 若不是因为两个因素，这也许会是一个合理的假设。
[01:29.37]First the electroencephalograph (which is simply a device for recording the electrical activity of the brain by attaching electrodes to the scalp) | 首先，脑电图仪（它不过是一种把电极贴在头皮上记录大脑电活动的装置）
[01:40.75]shows that while there is a change in the pattern of activity during sleep, | 表明，虽然睡眠期间活动的模式有变化，
[01:45.80]there is no evidence that the total amount of activity is any less. | 却没有证据表明活动的总量有任何减少。
[01:51.36]The second factor is more interesting and more fundamental. | 第二个因素更有趣，也更根本。
[01:56.00]Some years ago an American psychiatrist named William Dement published experiments dealing with the recording of eye-movements during sleep. | 几年前，一位名叫威廉·德门特的美国精神病学家发表了记录睡眠期间眼球运动的实验结果。
[02:06.91]He showed that the average individual's sleep cycle is punctuated with peculiar bursts of eye-movements, some drifting and slow, others jerky and rapid. | 他指出，普通人的睡眠周期被一阵阵奇怪的眼球运动打断，有的缓慢而漂移，有的急促而抽动。
[02:20.33]People woken during these periods of eye-movements generally reported that they had been dreaming. | 在这些眼球运动期间被唤醒的人普遍报告说他们一直在做梦。
[02:26.91]When woken at other times they reported no dreams. | 在其他时间被唤醒的人则说没有做梦。
[02:30.85]If one group of people were disturbed from their eye-movement sleep for several nights on end, | 如果一组人连续几夜在眼球运动睡眠中被打扰，
[02:36.30]and another group were disturbed for an equal period of time but when they were not exhibiting eye-movements, | 而另一组人在相等的时间里、在他们没有出现眼球运动时被打扰，
[02:45.44]the first group began to show some personality disorders while the others seemed more or less unaffected. | 第一组人开始表现出某些人格失调，而另一组人则似乎或多或少不受影响。
[02:54.88]The implications of all this were that it was not the disturbance of sleep that mattered, but the disturbance of dreaming. | 所有这一切意味着，重要的不是睡眠受到打扰，而是做梦受到打扰。
